      "knowledge_point": "Rewriting an Expression Using the Quotient and Power Rules of Logarithms",
      "kp_id": "e-4083"
    },
    "q-79403": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79403",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Express $4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)$ as a single logarithm.",
      "worked_solution": "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)+{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(ab)$ - the product rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡(\\frac{a}{b})$ - the quotient rule\n\n- ${log}_{n}⁡({c}^{m})=m{log}_{n}⁡(c)$ - the power rule\n\nUsing the power rule and then the quotient rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
